### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to check whether a string is a palindrome using the Stream API. This tests IntStream.range, allMatch with short-circuiting, and input normalization.

### How do you check a palindrome with IntStream?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class PalindromeCheck {
    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        return IntStream.range(0, n / 2)
            .allMatch(i -> s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(n - 1 - i));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code1 = "ABCBA";
        String code2 = "SAV2001";

        System.out.println(code1 + " palindrome? " + isPalindrome(code1));
        System.out.println(code2 + " palindrome? " + isPalindrome(code2));
    }
}
```

```text
ABCBA palindrome? true
SAV2001 palindrome? false
```

### Why do you only iterate half the string?

Once the first half matches the mirrored second half, the entire string is confirmed. A single middle character in an odd-length string mirrors itself and needs no check. Iterating the full string would compare each pair twice.

```java
// String: "ABCBA" (length 5)
// range(0, 2) generates indices 0 and 1
// i=0: 'A' == 'A' (index 4) -> match
// i=1: 'B' == 'B' (index 3) -> match
// Middle 'C' at index 2 mirrors itself -> no check needed
// Result: true
```

### How does allMatch short-circuit?

`allMatch` stops processing the moment it finds a mismatch. For a string like "SAV2001", the first comparison fails because 'S' does not equal '1', so no further indices are checked.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class ShortCircuitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "SAV2001";
        int n = code.length();

        boolean result = IntStream.range(0, n / 2)
            .peek(i -> System.out.println(
                "Checking index " + i + ": '" + code.charAt(i)
                + "' vs '" + code.charAt(n - 1 - i) + "'"))
            .allMatch(i -> code.charAt(i) == code.charAt(n - 1 - i));

        System.out.println("Palindrome? " + result);
    }
}
```

```text
Checking index 0: 'S' vs '1'
Palindrome? false
```

Only one comparison ran before short-circuiting.

### How do you normalize input for real-world checks?

Real palindrome checks ignore case, spaces, and punctuation.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class NormalizedPalindrome {
    public static boolean isPalindrome(String input) {
        String cleaned = input.toLowerCase()
            .replaceAll("[^a-z0-9]", "");
        int n = cleaned.length();
        return IntStream.range(0, n / 2)
            .allMatch(i -> cleaned.charAt(i) == cleaned.charAt(n - 1 - i));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("Was It A Car Or A Cat I Saw"));
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("A1B2B1A"));
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("SAV-2001"));
    }
}
```

```text
true
true
false
```

### How is this different from reverse-and-compare?

| Approach | Allocations | Short-circuits | Time |
|---|---|---|---|
| IntStream allMatch | None | Yes, on first mismatch | O(n/2) worst case |
| Reverse and compare | New StringBuilder + String | No, always reverses full string | O(n) always |

```java
// Reverse-and-compare approach
boolean isPalindromeReverse(String s) {
    return s.equals(new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString());
}
// Works but always processes the full string and allocates
```

The allMatch approach is more efficient because it stops at the first mismatch and allocates nothing.

### What are the edge cases?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class PalindromeEdgeCases {
    static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        return IntStream.range(0, n / 2)
            .allMatch(i -> s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(n - 1 - i));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + isPalindrome(""));
        System.out.println("Single: " + isPalindrome("A"));
        System.out.println("Two same: " + isPalindrome("AA"));
        System.out.println("Two diff: " + isPalindrome("AB"));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Single: true
Two same: true
Two diff: false
```

Empty and single-character strings are palindromes by definition. The half-range `range(0, 0)` produces no indices, and `allMatch` on an empty stream returns true.

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: not normalizing case before checking.

```java
// WRONG: 'A' != 'a', so "Aba" fails
boolean wrong = isPalindrome("Aba");  // false!

// CORRECT: normalize first
String cleaned = "Aba".toLowerCase();
boolean correct = isPalindrome(cleaned);  // true
```

Mistake: using `range(0, n)` instead of `range(0, n/2)`.

```java
// WRONG: checks every pair twice, wasting half the comparisons
IntStream.range(0, n)
    .allMatch(i -> s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(n - 1 - i));

// CORRECT: only the first half
IntStream.range(0, n / 2)
    .allMatch(i -> s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(n - 1 - i));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find the longest palindromic substring?" Use dynamic programming or expand-around-center.
- "How do you check if a number is a palindrome?" Convert to string and apply the same check, or reverse digits mathematically.
- "Can you use this to validate account codes?" Yes, the bank can flag overly predictable palindromic reference codes as weak.
